[{"@type": "Comment", "datePublished": "2025-11-27T12:42:27.375Z", "text": "👌🏽", "author": {"@type": "Person", "name": "MJ Baah"}, "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/LikeAction", "userInteractionCount": 0}}, {"@type": "Comment", "datePublished": "2025-11-26T06:23:19.823Z", "text": "Well said sir, more needs to be done to ensure a just and accessible energy transition within the shores of local communities in Africa. however the 3 points you listed out remains a significant tool in addressing energy challenges in Africa. thank you for sharing these insights info. #3xrenewables #IRENA,#AfricaEnergy #RenewableEnergy #EnergyAcess #JustEnergy 💨🌊☀️", "author": {"@type": "Person", "name": "Samuel Chidubem"}, "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/LikeAction", "userInteractionCount": 1}}, {"@type": "Comment", "datePublished": "2025-11-27T19:51:17.159Z", "text": "Great analysis, but one essential point is missing:

The challenge of energy access in Africa is not only about infrastructure or the cost of capital.

What is often overlooked is this:
1. even when electricity reaches households, the economic model is not adapted to local realities;

2. households do not consume because tariffs do not reflect their real income levels;

3. national systems are not always designed to ensure stable and reliable service;

4. and the approach remains too “top-down,” while success also relies on local solutions (mini-grids, home solar, storage, hybrid systems, etc.).

In other words:
Electrification is not only a matter of financing and infrastructure, but of technical and economic models adapted to the realities on the ground.

This is the missing point in the text and it completely changes the understanding of the issue.", "author": {"@type": "Person", "name": "Dr.Mariama Said Mohamed"}, "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/LikeAction", "userInteractionCount": 0}}, {"@type": "Comment", "datePublished": "2025-11-26T16:02:01.896Z", "text": "Well said Riad Meddeb ! Sustainable energy acceSs is not a purpose by itself, it matters only if it delivers development, equity and resilience. The affordability gap is what jeopoadizes Africa from benefitting from a sustainable energy transition that has the potential to transform continent future economies. Glad to see UNDP Sustainable Energy Hub pointing in the right direction: M300 will only be successful if we transform connectivity targets into measurable development outcomes.", "author": {"@type": "Person", "name": "Andrea Renzulli"}, "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/LikeAction", "userInteractionCount": 1}}]

